READING
  * Every parent satisfies the SF-1 predicate: class(dense) != class(dilute).
  * Parent A reproduces SF-1: D -> (z=1, soft {0, pi}); D-1/4 -> (1, {0, pi/2}); E -> (2, {0}).
    D and D-1/4 share the class but have different edge positions and velocities (2 vs sqrt2):
    the class is a strict compression of the edge-data set, so it cannot fix those values.
  * Parent B: same predicate, but the dilute edge has r_e = 4 -> z = 4, class (4, 1) != (2, 1).
  * Parent C: two pockets. The dense sector has 4 occupation edges with two distinct |velocities|
    (one v_b cannot describe it) and more soft momenta; the dilute sector has n_soft = 2 (0 and 2k0).
  => same earned predicate, different edge data, different effective-law class: E-13 does not FIX.
